    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
      <h2>Defense operations demand zero tolerance for failure.</h2>
      <p>Military installations, logistics networks, and mission-critical facilities operate under conditions that commercial software was never designed for — classified environments, disconnected operations, and compliance frameworks that carry legal consequences.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div>
        <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--outlined"><img src="/static/img/icons/disconnected.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></div>
        <h3 class="h4">Disconnected operations</h3>
        <p>Forward operating locations, hardened facilities, and flightline areas frequently operate without network connectivity. Maintenance, inspections, and reporting must function completely offline — and sync when connectivity returns.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--outlined"><img src="/static/img/icons/readiness.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></div>
        <h3 class="h4">Asset readiness</h3>
        <p>Command requires real-time FMC/PMC/NMC status across all mission-critical systems — vehicles, power, communications, parts, and tools. A single untracked maintenance tool can compromise a mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--outlined"><img src="/static/img/icons/manual-workflows.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></div>
        <h3 class="h4">Manual workflows</h3>
        <p>Work orders, inspections, maintenance, oversight, and reporting across distributed operations need a single operational picture — today, logistics lacks one and runs on manual effort.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Base operation</p>
      <h2>Every installation. Fully accountable. Modern.</h2>
      <p>Military installations are complex operating environments — dozens of buildings, thousands of assets, contractors, and continuous readiness obligations. Assetas connects each step.</p>
      <img class="cover-image portrait-image" src="/static/img/defense/rope-and-carabiner.webp" width="420" height="478" alt="A metal carabiner linking two ropes" loading="lazy">
    </div>
    <ol class="points">
      <li><h3>Installation asset registry</h3><p>Every facility, utility system, vehicle, and piece of equipment registered with live condition status, maintenance history, and warranty data — searchable by building number, system type, or mission criticality.</p></li>
      <li><h3>DPW work order management</h3><p>Service orders issued, tracked, and closed digitally — from routine PM to emergency repairs. Contractor performance tracked against IDIQ and MATOC task orders automatically.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Utility systems monitoring</h3><p>Real-time monitoring of electrical distribution, water, natural gas, and steam systems across the installation. Outage alerts surface before failures cascade into mission impact.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Offline-capable field execution</h3><p>Maintenance crews working in hardened facilities or disconnected environments execute work orders and file inspection records offline. Full sync when connectivity is restored — nothing lost.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Logistics</p>
      <h2>Parts where they need to be. When they need to be there.</h2>
      <p>NMC assets waiting on parts represent the single largest avoidable drag on readiness. Assetas connects maintenance requirements, parts inventory, and supply chain status — so commanders know what is NMC, why, and when it will be back.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="points points--single">
      <li><h3>Parts &amp; inventory management</h3><p>NSN-based inventory tracking, bench stock visibility, and shop stock list management — integrated with unit maintenance operations and connected to DLA supply chain data.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Readiness-to-supply linkage</h3><p>Every NMC asset linked to its outstanding parts requisition. Commanders see — at a glance — what is down, what part is needed, and when it arrives.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Supply chain visibility</h3><p>EDD tracking on all open requisitions, backorder status, and substitute NSN identification — surfaced at the unit level without manual status calls to the supply room.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

